\subsection{GET, PUT and POST queries}

To make things easier, we will add four methods: [[get_query]], [[put_query]], 
[[post_query]] and [[del_query]], which are shortcuts to make GET, PUT, POST 
and DEL queries to LADOK.

We note that we must record the time of the last GET request to track the 
freshness of the XSRF token.
The other requests track that by the use of the XSRF token itself.
We'll get back to this in \cref{XSRFtoken}.

A successful HTTP status does not guarantee that we actually received LADOK
JSON. When an authenticated session expires, LADOK may redirect us to an HTML
login page and still finish with [[200 OK]]. If we were to call
[[response.json()]] unconditionally in that situation, the user would only see
a low-level decoding failure. We therefore decode successful responses through a
small helper that raises [[LadokAPIError]] with the final URL and content type
when the body is not JSON.

The raw-text case needs one extra wrinkle.  The [[weblogin]] session wrapper is
helpful for JSON endpoints because it can inspect HTML responses and trigger a
login flow when LADOK silently redirects us.  But the same wrapper also feeds
all text responses to its HTML detectors, and XML with an encoding declaration
causes [[lxml.html]] to raise before our code gets the body.  We therefore let
[[get_query_text]] force authentication with an ordinary JSON request first,
then make the raw request through [[requests.Session.request(self.session,
...)]]: that still uses the 
same authenticated [[self.session]] object (same cookies, same MFA-backed
login), but bypasses [[AutologinSession.request]]'s HTML detection layer.
<<functions>>=
def response_json_or_error(
    response: requests.Response, path: str, method: str) -> dict | list:
  """Return JSON from [[response]] or raise contextual [[LadokAPIError]]."""
  try:
    return response.json()
  except ValueError as err:
    content_type = response.headers.get("content-type", "<missing>")
    body = " ".join(response.text.split())
    if len(body) > 120:
      body = body[:117] + "..."
    raise LadokAPIError(
      f"{method} request to {path} returned non-JSON response "
      f"from {response.url} (Content-Type: {content_type}): {body}") from err
@

Let's verify the improved error reporting with a synthetic HTML login page. The
helper should raise [[LadokAPIError]] rather than leaking a bare JSON decoding
exception.
<<test functions>>=
def test_response_json_or_error_reports_html_login_pages() -> None:
  class Response:
    url = "https://start.ladok.se/gui/loggain"
    headers = {"content-type": "text/html;charset=UTF-8"}
    text = "<html><title>Ladok - start</title></html>"

    def json(self):
      raise ValueError("not json")

  try:
    ladok3.response_json_or_error(Response(), "/studentinformation", "GET")
  except ladok3.LadokAPIError as err:
    assert "non-JSON response" in str(err)
    assert "text/html;charset=UTF-8" in str(err)
    assert "https://start.ladok.se/gui/loggain" in str(err)
  else:
    assert False
@

<<LadokSession data methods>>=
def get_query(self, path: str,
              content_type: str = "application/vnd.ladok-resultat+json"
              ) -> dict | list:
  """
  Make GET query to LADOK server and return JSON data.
  
  Args:
    path: API endpoint path
    content_type: HTTP Content-Type header value
  
  Returns:
    JSON data from the response
    
  Raises:
    LadokServerError: If the server returns an error message
    LadokAPIError: If the request fails or returns an error status
  """
  headers = self.headers.copy()
  headers["Content-Type"] = content_type

  <<record time of request>>

  response = self.session.get(
    url=self.base_gui_proxy_url + path,
    headers=headers)
     
  if response.ok:
    return response_json_or_error(response, path, "GET")
  try:
    error_msg = response.json()["Meddelande"]
    raise LadokServerError(error_msg)
  except:
    error_msg = response.text
  raise LadokAPIError(f"GET request to {path} failed: {error_msg}")

def get_query_text(self, path: str, accept: str) -> str:
  """
  Make GET query to LADOK and return the raw response text.

  Use this when an endpoint serves a non-JSON representation (e.g.\
  XML) that we want to hand to the caller for parsing.  The Accept
  header is set explicitly because [[self.headers]] only advertises
  the JSON variants.

  Args:
    path:   API endpoint path
    accept: HTTP Accept header value, e.g.
            'application/xml, text/xml;q=0.9'

  Returns:
    response.text from the LADOK response

  Raises:
    LadokServerError: If the server returns an error message
    LadokAPIError: If the request fails or returns an error status
  """
  headers = self.headers.copy()
  headers.pop("Content-Type", None)
  headers["Accept"] = accept

  self.user_info_JSON()
  <<record time of request>>

  response = requests.Session.request(
    self.session,
    "GET",
    url=self.base_gui_proxy_url + path,
    headers=headers)

  if response.ok:
    if "html" in response.headers.get("content-type", "").lower():
      raise LadokAPIError(
        f"GET request to {path} returned unexpected HTML from "
        f"{response.url}")
    return response.text
  try:
    error_msg = response.json()["Meddelande"]
    raise LadokServerError(error_msg)
  except:
    error_msg = response.text
  raise LadokAPIError(f"GET request to {path} failed: {error_msg}")

def put_query(self, path: str, put_data: dict | list,
              content_type: str = "application/vnd.ladok-resultat+json"
              ) -> dict | list:
  """
  Make PUT query to LADOK server and return JSON data.
  
  Args:
    path: API endpoint path
    put_data: Data to send in request body
    content_type: HTTP Content-Type header value
  
  Returns:
    JSON data from the response
    
  Raises:
    LadokServerError: If the server returns an error message
    LadokAPIError: If the request fails or returns an error status
  """
  headers = self.headers.copy()
  headers["Content-Type"] = content_type
  headers["X-XSRF-TOKEN"] = self.xsrf_token
  headers["Referer"] = self.base_gui_url

  response = self.session.put(
    url=self.base_gui_proxy_url + path,
    json=put_data,
    headers=headers)
     
  if response.ok:
    return response_json_or_error(response, path, "PUT")
  try:
    error_msg = response.json()["Meddelande"]
    raise LadokServerError(error_msg)
  except:
    error_msg = response.text
  raise LadokAPIError(f"PUT request to {path} failed: {error_msg}")

def post_query(self, path: str, post_data: dict | list,
               content_type: str = "application/vnd.ladok-resultat+json"
               ) -> dict | list:
  """
  Make POST query to LADOK server and return JSON data.
  
  Args:
    path: API endpoint path
    post_data: Data to send in request body
    content_type: HTTP Content-Type header value
  
  Returns:
    JSON data from the response
    
  Raises:
    LadokServerError: If the server returns an error message
    LadokAPIError: If the request fails or returns an error status
  """
  headers = self.headers.copy()
  headers["Content-Type"] = content_type
  headers["X-XSRF-TOKEN"] = self.xsrf_token
  headers["Referer"] = self.base_gui_url

  response = self.session.post(
    url=self.base_gui_proxy_url + path,
    json=post_data,
    headers=headers)
     
  if response.ok:
    return response_json_or_error(response, path, "POST")
  try:
    error_msg = response.json()["Meddelande"]
    raise LadokServerError(error_msg)
  except:
    error_msg = response.text
  raise LadokAPIError(f"POST request to {path} failed: {error_msg}")

def del_query(self, path: str) -> bool | dict | list | requests.Response:
  """
  Returns DELETE query response for path on the LADOK server.

  Args:
      path (str): API endpoint path

  Returns:
    True, if success but no content is returned.
    JSON data from the response, if any.
    Otherwise the response object.

  Raises:
      LadokServerError: If the server returns an error message
      LadokAPIError: If the request fails or returns an error status
  """
  headers = self.headers.copy()
  headers["X-XSRF-TOKEN"] = self.xsrf_token

  response = self.session.delete(url=self.base_gui_proxy_url+path,
                                 headers=headers)

  if response.status_code == requests.codes.no_content:
    return True
  if response.ok:
    try:
      return response.json()
    except:
      return response
  try:
    error_msg = response.json()["Meddelande"]
    raise LadokServerError(error_msg)
  except:
    error_msg = response.text
  raise LadokAPIError(f"DELETE request to {path} failed: {error_msg}")
@

\subsection{The XSRF token}\label{XSRFtoken}

We note that the PUT, POST and DEL queries require an XSRF token.
They all get it through [[self.xsrf_token]].
We provide a property for that.
LADOK sets the XSRF token when we make our first request.
If we haven't made any request, we don't have one.
Also, if too long time passes between requests, the existing XSRF token will be 
invalidated by LADOK.
<<LadokSession data methods>>=
@property
def xsrf_token(self) -> str:
  """
  Get a fresh XSRF token for authenticated requests.

  LADOK requires XSRF tokens for PUT, POST, and DELETE requests. This property
  ensures the token is fresh and valid, triggering a re-login if necessary.

  Returns:
      str: A valid XSRF token for use in authenticated requests.
  """
  <<ensure the XSRF token is fresh>>

  cookies = self.session.cookies.get_dict()
  return cookies["XSRF-TOKEN"]
@

Now, we must ensure there is a fresh XSRF token.
There are two cases:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
If we don't have an XSRF token, this means that we must log in to get one.
Since we use [[weblogin]], we'll simply perform a GET request, \ie one that 
doesn't require an XSRF token.
\item
If we have one, it might be expired due to a timeout.
In this case, we must log in again to get a new one.
\end{enumerate}
This means that we want to keep track of when the last request happened, so we 
can determine if our XSRF token is fresh or not.
<<LadokSession constructor body>>=
self.__access_time = None
self.__timeout = datetime.timedelta(minutes=15)
<<record time of request>>=
self.__access_time = datetime.datetime.now()
@ We note here that we assume that whenever the XSRF token is requested, a PUT, 
POST or DEL request will soon follow.
Hence, we can update the time of the last request whenever the XSRF token is 
read.
